  "reason": "The records collectively point toward temperature-controlled aging in Al-Co-Cr-Fe-Ni-type alloys: R1 identifies strong decomposition and hardening near 600 °C, while R7 shows that gamma-prime/L12-type precipitate fractions can decline at higher annealing temperatures. R8 independently connects FCC/B2 interface structure and transition layers to strain localization and interfacial cracking, and R19 supplies related cross-slip-locking physics. Thus an expert could anticipate that aging-dependent ordering and interface evolution might produce a non-monotonic ductility response. However, none of the records discloses the specific combination of near-stoichiometric L12 ordering, elevated APBE and modulus, jagged FCC/B2 boundary tips, and higher-temperature ductile transition zones. Reconstructing that coupled mechanism and the precise 600 °C embrittlement/750 °C recovery sequence would require a substantial new idea."
